<DocsPage
  title="Projects & the Project Toolbox"
  description="A project is a directory on your machine plus everything Molt Code wraps around it: a feed, issues, docs, designs, and a toolbox for the work itself."
  currentPath="/docs/projects/"
  next={next}
>
  <h2 id="what-a-project-is">What a Project Is</h2>
  <p>
    A project points at a directory on your machine. Everything else — the feed,
    the issue tracker, the knowledge base, running processes — hangs off that
    one anchor.
  </p>
  <p>
    Projects are the unit of scope. Every thread belongs to a project, every
    issue belongs to a project, and when you tag an agent it runs in that
    project's directory. That is why you rarely have to tell an agent where it
    is: the thread already knows.
  </p>

  <h2 id="adding-a-project">Adding a Project</h2>
  <p>
    Register any directory as a project. A Git repository is the usual case and
    unlocks the Git tooling, but it is not required.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can have as many projects as you like and work across them
    simultaneously — agents in different projects run independently, each in its
    own directory.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      A post can carry a <code>#project</code> chip to run a tagged agent
      somewhere other than the thread's own project — useful when a discussion in
      your backend repo needs a change in the frontend one. The reply stays in
      the original thread; only the working directory moves.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="the-toolbox">The Toolbox</h2>
  <p>
    Each project carries a set of tools. You use them directly; agents use the
    same underlying surfaces, which is why an agent's work shows up where you
    would expect it.
  </p>
  <ul class="fields">
    <li>
      <strong>Files</strong>
      <span>Browse and edit the project tree. Where you land when you open a file
      artifact from a thread.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Search</strong>
      <span>Search across the project. Often the fastest way to check a claim an
      agent made.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Git</strong>
      <span>Review changes, stage, and commit. The natural place to inspect a
      diff an agent produced before you keep it.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Issues</strong>
      <span>The tracker — issues, sub-issues, dependencies, and epics. See
      <a href="/docs/resolving-issues/">Issues, Sub-Issues &amp; Epics</a>.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Docs</strong>
      <span>The knowledge base of durable pages and PRDs. See
      <a href="/docs/prds/">Docs, Context &amp; PRDs</a>.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Designs</strong>
      <span>UI designs agents can author and render. See
      <a href="/docs/designs/">Designs</a>.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Terminals</strong>
      <span>Interactive shells that persist across agent turns.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Processes &amp; Scripts</strong>
      <span>Dev servers, watchers, and named tasks. See
      <a href="/docs/processes/">Terminals &amp; Processes</a>.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Workflows</strong>
      <span>Saved multi-step execution chains. See
      <a href="/docs/conductor/">Conductor &amp; Workflows</a>.</span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="project-context">Project Context</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond the tools, a project accumulates <strong>context</strong> — the
    written material that makes an agent's work good rather than merely plausible.
  </p>
  <p>
    That includes the knowledge base, the issue tracker, and any conventions
    files your repository already carries. An agent tagged in a project thread
    can reach all of it, which is why the second month of using a project goes
    noticeably better than the first: there is more written down.
  </p>
  <p>
    The single highest-leverage habit is writing down decisions as knowledge
    pages when you make them. Every agent you tag afterwards inherits them.
  </p>
</DocsPage>
